# gemma4:12b-ctx4k — the fallback chain's last rung, made loadable.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (measured 2026-07-29, docs/HARDENING.md F38):
# The local rung had never once completed a task in production. Every attempt died with
# "llama-server reported out-of-memory during startup", and the obvious reading — a 7.6GB
# model on a 4GB RTX 3050 — was wrong. The allocation that actually fails is the KV CACHE,
# sized from the model's DEFAULT context window, not the weights. llama3.1 (4.9GB) failed
# the same way while asking for a 16,642,998,272-byte (15.5GB) buffer, which no amount of
# free VRAM on this machine was ever going to satisfy.
#
# Neither the hermes CLI nor orchestrator's ollama_chat() passes `num_ctx`, so Ollama used
# each model's full default context every time. Baking the cap into a model VARIANT fixes
# both callers at once and keeps the choice in config rather than code, which is the
# model-agnostic constraint in CLAUDE.md ("swapping a model = editing models.yaml, never
# code").
#
# WHY 4096 AND NOT 8192: both load, measured on this box —
#     num_ctx=4096 -> loads in  75s, 1.5 tok/s
#     num_ctx=8192 -> loads in 201s, 1.1 tok/s
# This rung's entire job is to COMPLETE work when cloud quota is exhausted rather than park
# it, so loading reliably beats context headroom. Free RAM here swung between 2.8GB and
# 7.9GB during a single hour of testing; the smaller cache is far likelier to fit whenever
# it is actually needed, and a rung that OOMs under memory pressure is decorative again.
#
# REBUILD:  ollama create gemma4:12b-ctx4k -f config/gemma4-12b-ctx4k.Modelfile

FROM gemma4:12b

PARAMETER num_ctx 4096
